  "narrative": "The nine differentially-abundant metabolites show a striking and highly specific enrichment for **Pyrimidine metabolism** (KEGG map00240; WikiPathways WP4022). The RaMP hypergeometric test returned an FDR of 0 with a fold enrichment of 878.7, with six of the nine input compounds—deoxyuridine (C00526), CDP (C00112), uridine 5′-monophosphate (C00105), dihydrouracil (C00429), ureidopropionic acid (C02642), and deoxyribose 1-phosphate (C00672)—matching pathway members. No other metabolic domain reached significance, firmly localising the treatment effect to the pyrimidine salvage and de novo synthesis network.\n\nWithin this pathway, the most interconnected drivers are **deoxyuridine** and **dihydrouracil**. Deoxyuridine sits upstream of dihydrouracil in the reductive catabolic branch (2-reaction path via dihydropyrimidine; confirmed reachable in the KEGG reaction graph). Elevated deoxyuridine alongside dihydrouracil accumulation is consistent with impaired dihydropyrimidinase activity, a pattern also observed in the SMPDB-annotated disorder “Dihydropyrimidinase Deficiency,” suggesting a bottleneck at the step catalysed by dihydropyrimidinase (DPD). **Uridine 5′-monophosphate (UMP)** and **CDP** are bidirectionally connected through the CTP-synthetic arm (UMP → UTP → CTP via CTP synthetase), with CDP elevation further implicating intensified nucleotide phosphorylation. **Ureidopropionic acid**, a direct product of dihydrouracil hydrolysis, also accumulates, reinforcing the catabolic overload hypothesis.\n\nBiologically, pyrimidine depletion or catabolic blockade impairs DNA/RNA synthesis, affecting rapidly proliferating cells, and can alter phospholipid and glycophospholipid biosynthesis through CDP-linked pathways. Spironolactone, a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, is also a known inhibitor of human DPD *in vitro*, providing a plausible mechanistic link between the treatment condition and the observed dihydropyrimidinase-related signature. The coordinated elevation of nucleoside/nucleotide catabolites (deoxyuridine, dihydrouracil, ureidopropionic acid) alongside increased CDP suggests a treatment-driven perturbation of pyrimidine homeostasis rather than a simple deficiency, consistent with drug-induced enzyme inhibition or transcriptional modulation of pyrimidine-catabolising genes.",
